[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I recall now. The Banach-Steinhaus theorem for Fréchet spaces says: if[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma family of continuous linear maps from a Fréchet space to a locally convex [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspace is pointwise bounded, then it is equicontinuous. This works for any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfamily, not just sequences. And the equicontinuity + pointwise convergence on [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact ⇒ uniform convergence works for nets too. So the result should hold for [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnets.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait, there's a subtlety. For the strong topology, we need uniform [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvergence on ALL bounded sets, not just on one. The argument above works for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meach bounded set individually, so it works for all bounded sets. So the result [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mholds.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I think the reason some references state this for sequences is that [0m
