[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also verify: is $\mathcal{E}(\mathbb{R})$ indeed Montel?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$C^\infty(\mathbb{R})$ with the topology of uniform convergence of all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mderivatives on compact sets is a nuclear Fréchet space. Nuclear Fréchet spaces [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare Schwartz spaces, and Schwartz Fréchet spaces are Montel spaces. So yes, $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathcal{E}(\mathbb{R})$ is Montel.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Montel property means: every closed bounded subset is compact. This is what [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwe need.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. $\mathcal{E}(\mathbb{R})$ is a nuclear Fréchet space, hence Montel.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. $T_n \to T$ weakly means $\langle T_n, \varphi \rangle \to \langle T, \varphi[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\rangle$ for all $\varphi \in \mathcal{E}$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. By Banach-Steinhaus (uniform boundedness for Fréchet spaces), $\{T_n - T\}$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis equicontinuous.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Let $B \subset \mathcal{E}$ be bounded. By Montel, $\overline{B}$ is compact.[0m[38;2;124;124;124m5. Equicontinuous family converging pointwise on a compact set converges [0m
[38;2;124;124;124muniformly (finite cover argument).[0m
